# expression-grammar — a recursive expression grammar with ORDERED CHOICE.
#
# A call's argument is either a nested call, a name, or a number:
#
#   return add(3, mul(4, 5))
#
# One capture type names three functions, tried left to right:
#
#   arg capture v call | name | num
#
# - `call | name | num`  — the first alternative that matches wins (PEG order);
# - each alternative is a library FUNCTION. A flat alternative is a `bare`
#   function with one capture (`name`, `num` below) — a built-in type name is
#   not allowed in the choice;
# - the grammar nests as deep as the engine's depth bound allows (64 captures,
#   31 call levels: depth 31 parses, 32 is refused), because `call` consumes a name and `(` BEFORE it
#   recurses — it is right-recursive, not left-recursive.
#
# ORDER MATTERS when an earlier alternative is permissive. `num` is declared
# `int`, which accepts a bare identifier at parse time, so `name` is listed
# first: `x` becomes a `name`, `3` falls through to `num`.
#
# See docs/library-authoring.md ("Ordered choice") and PROP-2026-0004.
extension txt

function ret
    arg literal "return"
    arg capture value call
    write `return ${value};
`
end

function call
    bare
    arg capture fname ident
    arg literal "("
    arg capture args operand* sep "," join ", "
    arg literal ")"
    write `${fname}(${args})`
end

# The choice. `sub[].func` in `capy ast --json` names the alternative that matched.
function operand
    bare
    arg capture v call | name | num
    write `${v}`
end

function name
    bare
    arg capture id ident
    write `${id}`
end

function num
    bare
    arg capture n int
    write `${n}`
end
